“I fanned the Babe once on an inside high pitch that would have been called a ball.  Apparently that made Ruth mad; the next time up I tried to keep the ball under that paunch he was getting around the middle. It didn’t go quite as low as I wanted it. I can still see its [it?its was a typo] disappearing like an aspirin tablet over the right field wall,” recalled Washington Senators pitcher Syd Cohen when he pitched against Babe Ruth. This was the day that forever associated Syd a place in baseball history as the last pitcher to allow a home run and strikeout the Babe in a Yankee uniform. 

“He’s an athlete at heart and the easiest man to handle that I have ever come in contact with,” said manager Roy “Hardrock” Johnson about the 20-year-old Cohen in 1926. “Hardrock” Johnson was right. Syd Cohen was an athlete at heart, always involved in sports. He played basketball and [while not playing baseball he returned home to El Paso, where he] earned extra money officiating basketball during the off-season back home in El Paso. Cohen also coached local men’s and women’s basketball teams.  Later in life, he picked up golf and joined local bowling leagues.

Syd was quoted in Arizona sportswriter Abe Chanin’s column as saying: “Gosh, I hope I’ll be in baseball next year. It’s my life.” He played three seasons [years] with the Washington Senators –1934, 1936-1937 -- and played with and managed [with] numerous minor league teams until 1957.  He continued to give of his time coaching local sandlots teams in El Paso, playing on old-timers or benefit teams and serving as pitching coach for University of Texas at El Paso UTEP baseball team.  

Sydney Harry Cohen was born in Baltimore, Maryland, on May 7, 1906.  He was the middle of three siblings, each born two years apart from each other, older brother Andrew Cohen and younger sister Eva. His Jewish immigrant parents, Lena (née Dishart) from Russia and Manus (Mannie) from Lithuania, both spoke English.  The 1910 Census showed the family living close to Lena’s family [for two years] in the area of Norfolk, Virginia.   Manus left his career as a cigar-maker and opened a grocery store when the family moved to Virginia.

Shortly after that, the Cohens moved to the Mexican border town of El Paso, Texas, where Lena and Mannie separated.

